   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">The Government of Kiribati has approved and adopted a National Action Plan (NAP) on Reintegration of Returned Migrant Workers that has been formulated through a consultative process, and has agreed on priority activities for implementation within the action plan.  The NAP includes four overarching components: Social and Psychological Reintegration, Economic Reintegration, Empowerment and Leadership for Reintegration, and Overall Management of Return and Reintegration. The NAP will contribute to the implementation of labour standards, and includes specific focus on &#x2018;Empowerment of migrant workers through enhanced awareness on their rights&apos; and &#x2018;Legal empowerment of migrant workers upon return&apos;. Tripartite constituents contributed to the development, validation and approval of the NAP. In 2022 priority components of NAP will be implemented. 

The Kiribati Ministry of Employment and Human Resources has approved and is utilizing the Guidelines on Standard Employment Terms as a tool in order to inform decision making on engagement with labour mobility initiatives. The guidelines are captured in the report &quot;Towards establishing standard employment contracts for migrant workers from Tuvalu and Kiribati&quot; which Identifies standard terms that ILO Conventions and Recommendations indicate should be included in the contracts of migrant workers in the agriculture, fisheries, hospitality and maritime sectors; and 
It recommends content of the terms identified above based on international labour standards, together with information on minimum requirements under current employment law in Australia and New Zealand.
The guidance tool will contribute to the alignment between migrant worker contracts and both national and international standards. The guidelines also clarify the minimum legal employment and labour conditions for migrant workers in Australia and New Zealand. The ILO supported the development of the Kiribati Reintegration Action Plan. ILO reviewed the existing policy landscape and support services, and identified potential gaps that could be addressed through a Reintegration National Action Plan. A consultation workshop with the ILO tripartite constituents and social partners was held on 16th March 2021 to obtain strategic inputs to develop the draft Reintegration National Action Plan. The draft Reintegration National Action Plan was subsequently developed and validated with the Ministry of Employment and Human Resources. ILO facilitated a follow-up workshop with tripartite constituents to validate the action plan and identify priority activities for implementation.  

ILO provided technical assistance and oversight to the report on standard employment, which served as a bedrock of the Guidelines on Standard Employment Terms.The report was developed through consultation workshops and desk research organized and overseen by ILO.  This report provides guidance on standard employment contracts for migrant workers from Kiribati, with particular reference to Australia and New Zealand (being the main destination countries). The validation process for the Guidelines on Standards Employment Terms was also leveraged as an opportunity to train tripartite constituents on standard employment terms and minimum standards in relation to employment law in Australia and New Zealand.</narrative>
   </description>
